k) on X</p><p>A dominant paradigm these days in economics is “neo-institutionalism.” Pushed for decades by Douglass North (1920–2015; Nobel 1993) and now the orthodoxy at the World Bank. It says, like a recipe book, “Add institutions and stir.” Institutions such as sharecropping or land</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/deirdremcclosk/status/2046558567210348837?s=61">https://x.com/deirdremcclosk/status/2046558567210348837?s=61</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://arnoldkling.substack.com/p/public-choice-links-4232026">Public Choice Links, 4/23/2026</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/deirdremcclosk/status/2046558567210348837?s=61</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/deirdremcclosk/status/2046558567210348837?s=61</guid><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 11:03:55 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Tuesday assorted links - Marginal REVOLUTION]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/04/tuesday-assorted-links-565.html">https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/04/tuesday-assorted-links-565.html</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://arnoldkling.substack.com/p/public-choice-links-4232026">Public Choice Links, 4/23/2026</a></p>]]></description><link>https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/04/tuesday-assorted-links-565.html</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/04/tuesday-assorted-links-565.html</guid><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 11:03:55 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Britain needs to tidy its room]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p>According to the historian al-Tabari, upon conquering Jerusalem the Caliph Umar took it upon himself to tidy up the 35-acre area surrounding the Temple Mount. This had been left covered in rubbish by the Byzantine authorities, as a deliberate way of humiliating the Jews, and the new ruler is said to have begun to clear the garbage with his own hands, declaring ‘Oh people, do what am I doing!’ Ancient history is full of campaigns of mobilisation among the populace, with residents encouraged to take up defence of this city, but this must be the first (or one of the first) examples of a public litter-picking campaign…</p><p>URL: <a href="https://www.edwest.co.uk/p/britain-needs-to-tidy-its-room?utm_source=substack&utm_campaign=post_embed&utm_medium=web">https://www.edwest.co.uk/p/britain-needs-to-tidy-its-room?utm_source=substack&utm_campaign=post_embed&utm_medium=web</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://arnoldkling.substack.com/p/public-choice-links-4232026">Public Choice Links, 4/23/2026</a></p>]]></description><link>https://www.edwest.co.uk/p/britain-needs-to-tidy-its-room?utm_source=substack&amp;utm_campaign=post_embed&amp;utm_medium=web</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.edwest.co.uk/p/britain-needs-to-tidy-its-room?utm_source=substack&amp;utm_campaign=post_embed&amp;utm_medium=web</guid><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 11:03:55 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Did Institutions Spark Modern Economic Growth?]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p>Did institutions ignite modern prosperity, or did they merely stabilize forces already reshaping the economy? A test of the dominant theory of material progress…</p><p>URL: <a href="https://frompovertytoprogress.substack.com/p/did-institutions-spark-modern-economic?utm_source=substack&utm_campaign=post_embed&utm_medium=web">https://frompovertytoprogress.substack.com/p/did-institutions-spark-modern-economic?utm_source=substack&utm_campaign=post_embed&utm_medium=web</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://arnoldkling.substack.com/p/public-choice-links-4232026">Public Choice Links, 4/23/2026</a></p>]]></description><link>https://frompovertytoprogress.substack.com/p/did-institutions-spark-modern-economic?utm_source=substack&amp;utm_campaign=post_embed&amp;utm_medium=web</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://frompovertytoprogress.substack.com/p/did-institutions-spark-modern-economic?utm_source=substack&amp;utm_campaign=post_embed&amp;utm_medium=web</guid><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 11:03:55 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Government Doesn’t Have to Manage the Air]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p>Air travel in America is a mess right now. There are interminable lines at airport security, near misses or crashes on the ground and in the air, and the airports themselves remain overcrowded and antiquated. Although it is easy to blame short-term causes, such as the failure of Congress to appropriate money for the Transportation Security Administration (TSA), the bigger problem is the government is simply not good at running air travel…</p><p>URL: <a href="https://www.thefp.com/p/government-doesnt-have-to-manage-the-air?utm_source=substack&utm_campaign=post_embed&utm_medium=web">https://www.thefp.com/p/government-doesnt-have-to-manage-the-air?utm_source=substack&utm_campaign=post_embed&utm_medium=web</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://arnoldkling.substack.com/p/public-choice-links-4232026">Public Choice Links, 4/23/2026</a></p>]]></description><link>https://www.thefp.com/p/government-doesnt-have-to-manage-the-air?utm_source=substack&amp;utm_campaign=post_embed&amp;utm_medium=web</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.thefp.com/p/government-doesnt-have-to-manage-the-air?utm_source=substack&amp;utm_campaign=post_embed&amp;utm_medium=web</guid><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 11:03:55 GMT</pubDate></item></channel></rss>